1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,679
Every task on the RHCSA exam starts the same way.

2
00:00:03,699 --> 00:00:06,844
You get a shell prompt, and you type a command.

3
00:00:07,314 --> 00:00:12,405
If the syntax is off, nothing happens, or the wrong thing does. So in this video:

4
00:00:12,425 --> 00:00:16,318
access a shell prompt, and issue commands with correct syntax.

5
00:00:20,338 --> 00:00:22,921
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:23,391 --> 00:00:27,040
Access a shell prompt, and issue commands with correct syntax.

7
00:00:27,510 --> 00:00:30,391
We'll read the prompt, break a command into its parts,

8
00:00:30,411 --> 00:00:33,293
and learn to read the errors when the syntax is wrong.

9
00:00:40,313 --> 00:00:43,152
Every command starts at a prompt. Let's read ours.

10
00:00:43,622 --> 00:00:46,862
whoami says we're student. hostname says node1.

11
00:00:46,882 --> 00:00:49,706
echo dollar SHELL says our shell is bash.

12
00:00:49,726 --> 00:00:52,967
And pwd says we're in slash home slash student.

13
00:00:56,437 --> 00:00:59,617
Now look at the prompt itself. In square brackets:

14
00:00:59,637 --> 00:01:03,649
the user, an at sign, the host name, and the current directory.

15
00:01:03,669 --> 00:01:08,257
A tilde means your home directory. The dollar sign means a regular user.

16
00:01:08,277 --> 00:01:10,113
Root's prompt ends in a hash.

17
00:01:22,633 --> 00:01:27,221
Most commands follow one pattern: the command, then options, then arguments.

18
00:01:27,691 --> 00:01:31,436
The command is the program. Options change how it behaves.

19
00:01:31,456 --> 00:01:35,266
Arguments say what it works on. Spaces separate every part.

20
00:01:47,286 --> 00:01:52,685
Here, ls is the command, dash l is an option, and the file path is the argument.

21
00:01:56,655 --> 00:01:59,980
Short options use one dash. Long options use two.

22
00:01:59,999 --> 00:02:04,827
uname dash r, and uname dash dash kernel-release, give the same answer.

23
00:02:07,297 --> 00:02:08,832
Short options can be combined.

24
00:02:08,852 --> 00:02:11,164
ls dash l d means dash l and dash d together,

25
00:02:11,184 --> 00:02:14,274
and it lists the directories themselves, not their contents.

26
00:02:23,794 --> 00:02:28,531
Now let's break things on purpose, because on the exam you need to read errors fast.

27
00:02:29,001 --> 00:02:34,549
Capital L S: command not found. Linux is case-sensitive, and commands are lowercase.

28
00:02:38,519 --> 00:02:40,254
ls dash l with no space:

29
00:02:40,274 --> 00:02:45,006
bash looks for a command literally named ls-l, and can't find it.

30
00:02:49,976 --> 00:02:55,737
ls dash z: invalid option. The message even tells you to try ls dash dash help.

31
00:03:01,757 --> 00:03:05,044
A path that doesn't exist: no such file or directory.

32
00:03:08,514 --> 00:03:11,471
mkdir with nothing after it: missing operand.

33
00:03:11,491 --> 00:03:14,382
An operand is an argument the command needs.

34
00:03:18,852 --> 00:03:23,246
After every command, the exit status is stored in dollar question mark.

35
00:03:23,266 --> 00:03:26,789
Zero means success. Anything else means something failed.

36
00:03:33,759 --> 00:03:39,072
127 means command not found. The last command worked, so it gives zero.

37
00:03:44,592 --> 00:03:46,152
Spaces matter in another way, too.

38
00:03:46,172 --> 00:03:48,523
touch my file creates two files, named my and file,

39
00:03:48,543 --> 00:03:50,289
because the space separates arguments.

40
00:03:57,759 --> 00:04:01,323
Put quotes around the name, and you get one file named my file.

41
00:04:07,343 --> 00:04:10,287
When you forget syntax, the command can tell you.

42
00:04:10,307 --> 00:04:12,464
Most commands accept dash dash help.

43
00:04:17,934 --> 00:04:21,701
And the manual, run with man, is installed on this system.

44
00:04:21,721 --> 00:04:26,598
For what documentation is available in the exam, check Red Hat's exam page.

45
00:04:26,618 --> 00:04:27,642
We won't assume.

46
00:04:32,662 --> 00:04:36,290
Our five steps are: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

47
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:41,407
This objective changes nothing on the system, so there is nothing to persist,

48
00:04:41,427 --> 00:04:44,377
and we skip the reboot. Later videos will use it.

49
00:04:49,897 --> 00:04:55,657
Your turn. In your home directory, create a directory called lab1, and inside it,

50
00:04:55,677 --> 00:04:58,154
one empty file named notes dot txt.

51
00:05:01,124 --> 00:05:05,879
Then list it with a long listing. Three broken attempts are in the description.

52
00:05:05,899 --> 00:05:07,995
For each one, explain why it fails.

53
00:05:10,965 --> 00:05:13,782
Run the checker, and try before you open the solution.

54
00:05:18,302 --> 00:05:21,909
Next up: Log in and switch users in multi-user targets.

55
00:05:22,379 --> 00:05:25,281
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
